  "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,\\,{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\\,{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=5-5=0 & \u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- The set $V$ cannot be orthogonal since it contains  $3$ vectors from ${R}^{2}$ (in which we can have an orthogonal set with at most $2$ non-zero vectors). So, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=4+0-4=0 & \u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\".",
